package cosmonic:agent@0.4.3;

/// Where a session stands, as its host sees it. One set of states that every
/// host reports the same way, so a desktop app, a cluster's session API and a
/// component that drives an agent all say the same thing about one session.
///
/// The host owns the state. It derives it only from what the host itself can
/// vouch for: whether a run or a compaction is in progress, whether something
/// is held at one of the host's own gates waiting for a person, and how the
/// last run ended. Nothing an agent writes and nothing a model says sets it.
/// In particular, `needs-input` comes from the host's approval state, never
/// from a reply that asks the person a question: a run that finishes
/// `input-required` is `done`, and the person's answer starts the next run.
///
/// The host chooses the session, as it does for `session` and `run`: the
/// caller never names one. A host gives this import to a component that
/// drives or watches an agent (an adapter that serves a status endpoint, or
/// the driver of a child agent), bound to the session of the agent it
/// drives. An agent has no use for it on its own session.
///
/// The set of states will grow (archived is one under consideration). Adding
/// a case to `session-state` is a breaking change, so until a minor release
/// names a new state, a host reports it as `other` with the state's name.
/// A caller treats a state it does not know as unknown: it may show the name,
/// and acts on nothing.
///
/// Unstable behind `session-status` while hosts adopt it: the shape may
/// still change (for example, `needs-input` saying what is held) before it
/// is marked `@since`.
@unstable(feature = session-status)
interface session-status {
    /// One session's state. Exactly one holds at a time, checked in the order
    /// the cases are listed: a run that waits on a person is `needs-input`,
    /// not `working`.
    @unstable(feature = session-status)
    variant session-state {
        /// The host holds something for a person in this session: an approval
        /// at one of its gates, such as a tool call or an outbound request
        /// waiting to be allowed, a tool waiting to be added, or a change
        /// waiting for review. Whether a run is in progress or not.
        needs-input,
        /// A run or a compaction is in progress, and nothing is held for a
        /// person.
        working,
        /// Nothing is in progress and the last run ended with an error, or
        /// was cut off (the host stopped or restarted under it).
        failed,
        /// Nothing is in progress and the last run ended with a reply, for
        /// any finish (`done`, `input-required`, a limit, `refusal` or
        /// `content-filter`).
        done,
        /// Nothing is in progress, and no run has ended since the session
        /// began or was cleared, or the last run was cancelled.
        idle,
        /// A state this version does not name: its name, in kebab case.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A session's state, with when it began and the run it concerns.
    @unstable(feature = session-status)
    record status {
        state: session-state,
        /// When the session entered this state, in milliseconds since the
        /// Unix epoch. For `needs-input`, when the oldest thing still held
        /// was raised. `none` when the host does not know, such as an `idle`
        /// session that has never run.
        since-ms: option<u64>,
        /// The run the state concerns, by the `run-id` its driver gave: the
        /// run or compaction in progress for `working`, and for
        /// `needs-input` when one is in progress; the last run for `done`
        /// and `failed`. `none` otherwise.
        run: option<string>,
    }

    /// Why the host could not report the state.
    @unstable(feature = session-status)
    variant status-error {
        /// The host could not serve the call; the detail is for logs.
        unavailable(string),
        other(string),
    }

    /// The session's status now.
    @unstable(feature = session-status)
    get: async func() -> result<status, status-error>;

    /// The session's status now, then each change, in order. A reader that
    /// falls behind may miss a state that passed quickly, but never sees an
    /// older status after a newer one. Dropping the stream stops watching;
    /// the stream ends when the session does.
    @unstable(feature = session-status)
    watch: async func() -> result<stream<status>, status-error>;
}
